<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Orchicon.msi - the Windows installer.

  WHY AN MSI AT ALL. Wails emits an NSIS installer, and an NSIS .exe cannot be
  deployed by the tooling a Windows estate actually uses: Intune, SCCM, Group
  Policy and `msiexec /qn` all speak MSI, and none of them can drive an NSIS
  wizard. The NSIS installer stays (it is the desktop app's own installer, and
  `install.ps1 -Desktop` uses it); this packages the WHOLE product - CLI, TUI and
  desktop app - for the managed case.

  THE SHAPE, and each choice is a consequence of something measured:

  * PER-USER, NO ELEVATION (`Scope="perUser"`). Orchicon's whole model is "the
    plane and its services are host processes of the user who installs them",
    with a per-user data directory. A per-machine install (Program Files, an
    elevated service account) would put the BINARIES under one identity and run
    the SERVICES under another - the one arrangement the product does not
    support. Per-user also means no UAC prompt.

  * THE SERVICE BINARIES ARE NOT IN HERE (542 MB measured: otelcol 293, loki
    105, tempo 74, postgres 34, vm 24, nats 16). Bundling them would make a
    ~750 MB MSI that still has to pull multi-GB container images, and it would
    version-lock the services to the installer. `orchicon install` acquires them
    at setup time, which is what the one-line ps1 install does today.

  * THE SETUP RUNS `orchicon install` IN A VISIBLE CONSOLE, from the last step.
    NOT a custom action that talks to the operator: an MSI custom action has no
    console, so `installIsInteractive()` is false and any prompt writes into
    nothing and blocks for ever - the hazard install.ps1 documents at length.
    Delegating also means the MSI cannot drift from the CLI's notion of
    "installed": there is one implementation, and it is not here.

  * THE WIZARD'S ANSWERS TRAVEL AS ENVIRONMENT. `INSTANCE` and `RUNTIMEIMAGES`
    are PUBLIC properties (settable on the command line for `msiexec /qn`) and
    are handed to the wrapper as %1/%2. RUNTIMEIMAGES is the variable added in
    the same change - without it a wizard's multi-select was silently discarded
    by the non-interactive path, so an install fetched none of what was ticked.
-->
<!--
  BUILDING THIS FILE NEEDS TWO THINGS ON THE COMMAND LINE, and neither is implied by
  anything inside it:

      wix build Orchicon.wxs -arch x64 -ext WixToolset.UI.wixext/5.0.2

  * -ext ... : ui:WixUI is an EXTENSION element. The xmlns below makes the file
    well-formed and does NOT enable it (WIX0200).
  * the /5.0.2 suffix : the extension is restored from NuGet, and a bare package
    name does not resolve ("error WIX0144: The extension 'WixToolset.UI.wixext'
    could not be found"). It is versioned to match WIX_VERSION in the Makefile.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui">

  <Package Name="Orchicon"
           Manufacturer="Orchicon"
           Version="$(var.Version)"
           UpgradeCode="DF9438BD-B311-5C12-A1D2-EB415DF74F2D"
           Scope="perUser"
           Compressed="yes"
           InstallerVersion="500">

    <!-- A MAJOR UPGRADE REPLACES IN PLACE. Without this an install over an older
         version leaves two ARP entries and two sets of files, and the second
         `orchicon install` then has to guess which binary is "the" plane. -->
    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Orchicon is already installed." />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- PUBLIC PROPERTIES = the unattended surface. All-uppercase ids are
         settable from the command line, so an enterprise deployment is
             msiexec /i Orchicon.msi /qn INSTANCE=prod RUNTIMEIMAGES=1 SKIPSETUP=1
         and needs no wizard. -->
    <Property Id="INSTANCE" Value="dev" />
    <!-- NO Value: an empty Value attribute is illegal (WIX0006), and it must default to
         UNSET rather than "none" — the wrapper leaves the env var alone when %2 is
         empty, which is what keeps the interactive runtime-image prompt alive. A
         default of "none" would be an ANSWER, and the prompt would be skipped.
         Secure="yes" declares a property that carries a value from the UI or the
         command line without giving it one (and without being ignored — WIX1006). -->
    <Property Id="RUNTIMEIMAGES" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="SKIPSETUP" Value="0" />

    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="OrchiconIcon" />
    <!-- ARPNOMODIFY IS NOT DECLARED HERE. WixUI_InstallDir already defines it, and a
         second declaration is a hard error:
             error WIX0091: Duplicate Property with identifier 'ARPNOMODIFY' found.
         The UI set gives it, which is also the right value for this package. -->
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://orchicon.dev" />
    <Property Id="WIXUI_INSTALLDIR" Value="INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <!-- THE LICENSE PAGE TEXT IS *NOT* A PROPERTY. It is a BUILD-TIME variable:
             wix build ... -d WixUILicenseRtf=<path to the .rtf>
         The UI extension reads it then, embeds the file as a Binary and points the
         license control at that Binary. Declaring
             <Property Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="...\LICENSE.rtf" />
         looks like it should work and does NOTHING — the wizard kept rendering WiX's
         built-in placeholder, which is lorem ipsum. Reported from a real install:
         "the windows installer has some default text in there".
         Every build site now passes the variable; cmd/orchicon/windows_msi_test.go
         fails any of them that stops. -->

    <Icon Id="OrchiconIcon" SourceFile="$(var.SourceDir)\orchicon-desktop.ico" />

    <!-- PER-USER LOCATIONS ONLY. LocalAppDataFolder and the per-user Program Menu
         need no elevation; ProgramFilesFolder and the machine Program Menu would
         need both, and would put the binaries somewhere the installing user may
         not be able to write. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="LocalAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="ProgramsFolder" Name="Programs">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Orchicon" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Feature Id="CliFeature" Title="Orchicon (plane, CLI and TUI)" Level="1"
             Description="orchicon.exe and orch.exe: the control plane, the CLI and the TUI.">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="CliComponents" />
    </Feature>

    <Feature Id="DesktopFeature" Title="Orchicon desktop app" Level="1"
             Description="The GUI client. Optional — the web UI at the plane's URL does everything it does.">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="DesktopComponents" />
    </Feature>

    <ComponentGroup Id="CliComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <!-- KeyPath on the FILE (not the directory): per-user installs cannot use a
           directory as a key path, and a registry key path would add state to
           clean up for no benefit. -->
      <Component Id="OrchiconExe" Guid="*">
        <File Id="OrchiconExeFile" Source="$(var.SourceDir)\orchicon.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="OrchExe" Guid="*">
        <File Id="OrchExeFile" Source="$(var.SourceDir)\orch.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="SetupWrapper" Guid="*">
        <!-- The setup hand-off. Shipped as a file rather than an inline ExeCommand
             so the wizard's answers reach the child as %1/%2 with no nested quoting;
             see the header of orchicon-setup.cmd. -->
        <File Id="SetupCmdFile" Source="$(var.SourceDir)\orchicon-setup.cmd" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
      <Component Id="ReadmeFile" Guid="*">
        <File Id="ReadmeTxtFile" Source="$(var.SourceDir)\README-WINDOWS.txt" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="DesktopComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="DesktopExe" Guid="*">
        <File Id="DesktopExeFile" Source="$(var.SourceDir)\orchicon-desktop.exe" KeyPath="yes">
          <!-- Advertise="no" is REQUIRED for a per-user shortcut: an advertised
               shortcut is a machine-wide feature and fails without elevation. -->
          <Shortcut Id="StartMenuShortcut"
                    Directory="ProgramMenuFolder"
                    Name="Orchicon"
                    Description="Orchicon desktop app"
                    Advertise="no"
                    WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                    Icon="OrchiconIcon" />
          <Shortcut Id="DesktopShortcut"
                    Directory="DesktopFolder"
                    Name="Orchicon"
                    Description="Orchicon desktop app"
                    Advertise="no"
                    WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                    Icon="OrchiconIcon" />
        </File>
        <!-- The shortcuts are removed by their parent component's uninstall, so no
             explicit RemoveFolder is needed for them. -->
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- ===================== THE SETUP HAND-OFF =============================
         IMMEDIATE, NOT DEFERRED, and that is the load-bearing choice. A deferred
         custom action cannot read public properties - it only sees
         CustomActionData, which would mean a second property-setting action and
         a nested-quoting exercise to pass [INSTANCE] and [RUNTIMEIMAGES]
         through. An immediate action resolves them normally. Nothing here
         modifies the machine (it launches a detached process), so immediate is
         also the honest classification.

         Return="asyncNoWait": the MSI does not wait for the setup. Setup is
         minutes of downloads plus prompts a human answers - holding InstallFinalize
         open for that would make the wizard look hung and time out. The setup is
         idempotent and resumable, so losing the wait costs nothing.

         Impersonate="yes": it runs AS THE INSTALLING USER, not as SYSTEM,
         because everything it starts (the plane, postgres, nats) must belong to
         that user.

         `cmd /c start "title" ...` is what produces a VISIBLE console: a process
         spawned straight from a custom action inherits a hidden window, and the
         operator would see an install with no progress and no prompts. The first
         argument of `start` is the window TITLE, which is why the wrapper path is
         quoted as the second argument.

         [#SetupCmdFile] IS THE MSI'S OWN PATH FOR THAT FILE, and it is not the same
         thing as pasting a directory onto a filename. [INSTALLFOLDER]orchicon-setup.cmd
         ASSUMES the folder the wizard ended up using is the one this action thinks it
         is; a formatter for a File key asks the database where the file actually went.
         Reported from a real install: the wizard reached Finish, `start` could not find
         the wrapper and Windows raised "Windows cannot find
         C:\Users\...\Orchicon\orchicon-setup.cmd" — on a package whose cab provably
         contains it. A path built from a directory is the one thing here that can
         disagree with the install while looking right. -->
    <CustomAction Id="LaunchSetup"
                  Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                  Execute="immediate"
                  Impersonate="yes"
                  Return="asyncNoWait"
                  ExeCommand="cmd.exe /c start &quot;Orchicon setup&quot; &quot;[#SetupCmdFile]&quot; &quot;[INSTANCE]&quot; &quot;[RUNTIMEIMAGES]&quot;" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!-- NOT REMOVE: never on uninstall. NOT Installed is deliberately absent:
           a repair or an upgrade should re-converge, and `orchicon install` is
           idempotent - it upgrades an existing instance rather than duplicating
           it. SKIPSETUP=1 is the unattended opt-out (binaries only).

           AFTER="InstallFinalize", NOT "InstallFiles" - AND THAT WAS THE BUG.

           MSI runs the execute sequence TWICE: an IMMEDIATE pass, which walks the
           sequence and builds the script, and a DEFERRED pass, which runs it and
           actually copies the files. An IMMEDIATE custom action runs in the
           immediate pass, so one placed After="InstallFiles" runs when InstallFiles
           has merely been SCHEDULED - the files are not on disk yet.

           Reported from a real install: the wizard reached Finish, the hand-off
           fired, and Windows raised "cannot find
           C:\Users\...\Orchicon\orchicon-setup.cmd". Every later inspection
           found the file there, at exactly that path, which is what made the report
           look contradictory: CI now installs the package and lists
           orchicon-setup.cmd (2428 bytes) in that directory. The file was never
           missing - the hand-off ran BEFORE it was written.

           InstallFinalize sits after the deferred script has completed, so by the
           time this fires the payload is committed. (A deferred action would also
           work, but a deferred one cannot read [INSTANCE]/[RUNTIMEIMAGES] without
           CustomActionData - see the custom action's own note - so the sequence
           placement is the right lever.) -->
      <Custom Action="LaunchSetup" After="InstallFinalize" Condition="NOT REMOVE AND SKIPSETUP &lt;&gt; &quot;1&quot;" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_InstallDir" />
  </Package>
</Wix>
